Audit this circuit for vulnerabilities:

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.0;

include "comparators.circom";

// Decoder: given inp in [0, w), set out[inp] = 1 and all other entries 0,
// and set success = 1. The "one-hot" output is consumed by callers as a
// selector mask.
template Decoder(w) {
    signal input inp;
    signal output out[w];
    signal output success;
    var lc = 0;

    for (var i = 0; i < w; i++) {
        // BUG: out[i] is assigned with <-- (hint only). The only constraint
        // emitted is the quadratic (inp - i) * out[i] === 0, which merely
        // forces out[i] = 0 whenever inp != i, but NEVER forces out[i] = 1
        // when inp == i. So a prover may set the matching out[i] to 0.
        out[i] <-- (inp == i) ? 1 : 0;
        out[i] * (inp - i) === 0;
        lc = lc + out[i];
    }

    // success is just the sum of the bits; with all bits 0 it becomes 0.
    lc ==> success;
}
```

Vulnerability: Decoder one-hot output is under-constrained (bogus `out`/`success` accepted)

Explanation: The `Decoder` template is supposed to produce a one-hot vector where `out[inp] = 1` and every other entry is 0. The only constraint it emits per element is `out[i] * (inp - i) === 0`. That constraint forces `out[i]` to 0 when `inp != i`, but it places NO lower bound on `out[i]` when `inp == i` (the factor `(inp - i)` is zero, so the product is trivially zero for ANY value of `out[i]`). Combined with `out[i]` being assigned via the hint operator `<--`, a malicious prover can set the matching `out[i]` to 0, which makes the running sum `lc` equal 0 and therefore `success = 0`. The circuit accepts this bogus witness without error, so integrators that read `out`/`success` can be fed a non-one-hot decoding.

Fix: Compute each output bit through an `IsZero(inp - i)` component and bind `out[i] <== isz[i].out`. `IsZero` constrains its output to be exactly 1 when the input is zero and 0 otherwise, which forces the correct one-hot encoding; additionally constrain `success === 1` so an in-range input must succeed.

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.0;

include "comparators.circom";

template Decoder(w) {
    signal input inp;
    signal output out[w];
    signal output success;
    var lc = 0;

    // FIX: derive each out[i] from an IsZero(inp - i) sub-component. IsZero
    // fully constrains its output to be exactly 1 iff its input is 0 and 0
    // otherwise, so out[i] is forced to the correct one-hot value and the
    // prover can no longer supply a bogus all-zero mask.
    component isz[w];
    for (var i = 0; i < w; i++) {
        isz[i] = IsZero();
        isz[i].in <== inp - i;
        out[i] <== isz[i].out;
        lc = lc + out[i];
    }

    lc ==> success;
    // success is now forced to 1 for any valid in-range inp.
    success === 1;
}
```

Audit this circuit for vulnerabilities:

```circom
pragma circom 2.1.9;

include "circomlib/comparators.circom";

// Verifies the zAccount input UTXO nullifier in ZSwapV1. BUG: the `enabled`
// signal of ForceEqualIfEnabled is wired to the private key. Setting
// zAccountUtxoInSpendPrivKey = 0 (a valid key deriving the neutral point (0,1))
// disables the nullifier check, so any zAccountUtxoInNullifier is accepted and
// the same zAccount UTXO can be spent repeatedly.
template NullifierCheckBug() {
    signal input zAccountUtxoInNullifier;
    signal input zAccountUtxoInSpendPrivKey;
    signal input computedNullifier;     // = zAccountUtxoInNullifierHasher.out

    component check = ForceEqualIfEnabled();
    check.in[0] <== zAccountUtxoInNullifier;
    check.in[1] <== computedNullifier;
    check.enabled <== zAccountUtxoInSpendPrivKey;   // BUG: gated on the priv key
}

component main {public [zAccountUtxoInNullifier]} = NullifierCheckBug();
```

Vulnerability: Nullifier verification can be disabled by setting the spend private key to zero

Explanation: The nullifier equality is enforced by `ForceEqualIfEnabled()` whose `enabled` input is wired to `zAccountUtxoInSpendPrivKey`. `ForceEqualIfEnabled` only applies its `in[0] === in[1]` check when `enabled != 0`. A prover can set `zAccountUtxoInSpendPrivKey = 0` -- which is itself a valid private key, deriving the neutral/infinity public key `(0, 1)` via `BabyPbk` -- so `enabled = 0` and the nullifier check is skipped entirely. With the check disabled, `zAccountUtxoInNullifier` may take any value, so the attacker can mint a fresh nullifier each time and spend the same zAccount input UTXO an unlimited number of times, defeating double-spend protection.

Fix: Never gate the nullifier verification on a prover-controlled value. Hard-code `check.enabled <== 1;` so the equality `zAccountUtxoInNullifier === computedNullifier` is always enforced.

```circom
pragma circom 2.1.9;

include "circomlib/comparators.circom";

template NullifierCheckFixed() {
    signal input zAccountUtxoInNullifier;
    signal input zAccountUtxoInSpendPrivKey;
    signal input computedNullifier;

    component check = ForceEqualIfEnabled();
    check.in[0] <== zAccountUtxoInNullifier;
    check.in[1] <== computedNullifier;
    // FIX: always enforce the nullifier check.
    check.enabled <== 1;
}

component main {public [zAccountUtxoInNullifier]} = NullifierCheckFixed();
```

Audit this circuit for vulnerabilities:

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.5;

include "circuits/pairing/bigint.circom";
include "circuits/pairing/fp.circom";

// Adds two points (a, b) on an elliptic curve over Fp (BLS12-381 base field,
// 7 limbs of 55 bits). The template ASSUMES a != b and computes the chord
// slope lambda = (b.y - a.y) / (b.x - a.x). It is used by G1Add.
//
// BUG: nothing constrains a != b. If a prover supplies a == b, then both
// numerator and denominator of lambda are zero, so the slope constraint
// 0 = 0 holds for ANY lambda, and the addition formula degenerates. A
// malicious prover can therefore perform point doubling where unequal-point
// addition was intended, forging aggregate public keys / signatures.
template EllipticCurveAddUnequal(n, k, p) {
    signal input a[2][k];
    signal input b[2][k];
    signal output out[2][k];

    // lambda = (b.y - a.y) * inv(b.x - a.x)   (mod p)
    component dy = BigSubModP(n, k);
    component dx = BigSubModP(n, k);
    for (var i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        dy.a[i] <== b[1][i]; dy.b[i] <== a[1][i]; dy.p[i] <== p[i];
        dx.a[i] <== b[0][i]; dx.b[i] <== a[0][i]; dx.p[i] <== p[i];
    }

    // lambda is a witnessed quotient; constrained via lambda * dx == dy.
    // When a == b, dx == dy == 0 and this reduces to 0 == 0 for any lambda.
    signal lambda[k];
    component check = BigMultModP(n, k);
    component prod = BigMultModP(n, k);
    for (var i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        lambda[i] <-- 0; // hint: computed off-circuit
        check.a[i] <== lambda[i]; check.b[i] <== dx.out[i]; check.p[i] <== p[i];
    }
    for (var i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        check.out[i] === dy.out[i]; // 0 == 0 when a == b: no real constraint
    }

    // ... remaining x3 / y3 formulas elided; they depend only on lambda ...
    for (var i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        out[0][i] <== a[0][i];
        out[1][i] <== a[1][i];
    }
}

component main = EllipticCurveAddUnequal(55, 7, [35747322042231467, 36025922209447795, 1084959616957103, 7925923977987733, 16551456537884751, 23443114579904617, 1829881462546425]);
```

Vulnerability: Point doubling in EllipticCurveAddUnequal allows signature forgery

Explanation: G1Add delegates to EllipticCurveAddUnequal, which computes the chord slope lambda = (b.y - a.y)/(b.x - a.x) under the unchecked assumption that the two G1 points a and b are distinct. The slope is witnessed (lambda <-- ...) and constrained only through lambda * (b.x - a.x) === (b.y - a.y). When a malicious prover supplies a == b, both b.x - a.x and b.y - a.y are zero, so that constraint degenerates to 0 === 0 and is satisfied by ANY lambda. The prover can therefore perform a point doubling exactly where unequal-point addition was expected, producing an aggregate public key / signature that the protocol never intended and breaking soundness of the light-client signature check.

Fix: Replace the call to EllipticCurveAddUnequal in G1Add with EllipticCurveAdd, which detects equal inputs and switches to the proper point-doubling formula, so the unequal-addition slope is never applied to a == b and the constraint can no longer collapse to 0 === 0.

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.5;

include "circuits/pairing/bigint.circom";
include "circuits/pairing/fp.circom";

// Generic EC point addition that explicitly handles the a == b case
// (point doubling) instead of silently degenerating. G1Add must call
// THIS template, not EllipticCurveAddUnequal.
template EllipticCurveAdd(n, k, p) {
    signal input a[2][k];
    signal input b[2][k];
    signal output out[2][k];

    // Detect whether the two input points are equal limb-by-limb.
    component xEq[k];
    component yEq[k];
    signal accX[k+1];
    signal accY[k+1];
    accX[0] <== 0;
    accY[0] <== 0;
    for (var i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        xEq[i] = IsEqual(); xEq[i].in[0] <== a[0][i]; xEq[i].in[1] <== b[0][i];
        yEq[i] = IsEqual(); yEq[i].in[0] <== a[1][i]; yEq[i].in[1] <== b[1][i];
        accX[i+1] <== accX[i] + (1 - xEq[i].out);
        accY[i+1] <== accY[i] + (1 - yEq[i].out);
    }
    component xAllEq = IsZero(); xAllEq.in <== accX[k];
    component yAllEq = IsZero(); yAllEq.in <== accY[k];
    // isEqual == 1 iff a == b, selecting the doubling formula below.
    signal isEqual;
    isEqual <== xAllEq.out * yAllEq.out;

    // Use the doubling slope (3x^2)/(2y) when isEqual == 1, otherwise the
    // chord slope (b.y - a.y)/(b.x - a.x). With this branch the unequal
    // formula is only ever applied to genuinely unequal points, so the
    // slope constraint can no longer collapse to 0 == 0.
    // ... slope selection and x3/y3 formulas elided ...
    for (var i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        out[0][i] <== a[0][i];
        out[1][i] <== a[1][i];
    }
}

component main = EllipticCurveAdd(55, 7, [35747322042231467, 36025922209447795, 1084959616957103, 7925923977987733, 16551456537884751, 23443114579904617, 1829881462546425]);
```

Audit this circuit for vulnerabilities:

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.0;

include "circomlib/circuits/comparators.circom";

// EpochKeyLite checks that the epoch-key nonce is within the allowed range
// using LessThan(8). LessThan assumes its inputs fit in 8 bits, but `nonce`
// is never range-checked, so a prover can pass a huge value such as p-1 that
// overflows the comparator and makes `nonce < EPOCH_KEY_NONCE_PER_EPOCH`
// return true even though nonce is out of range.
template EpochKeyLite(FIELD_COUNT) {
    var EPOCH_KEY_NONCE_PER_EPOCH = 3;

    signal input nonce;        // attacker-controlled, NOT range-checked
    signal output out;

    // BUG: nonce fed straight into LessThan(8) with no bit-length constraint.
    component nonceCheck = LessThan(8);
    nonceCheck.in[0] <== nonce;
    nonceCheck.in[1] <== EPOCH_KEY_NONCE_PER_EPOCH;
    nonceCheck.out === 1;

    out <== nonce;
}

component main = EpochKeyLite(255);
```

Vulnerability: Unconstrained input to LessThan(8) allows comparison overflow in EpochKeyLite

Explanation: `EpochKeyLite` enforces the epoch-key nonce bound with `LessThan(8)`, but `LessThan(n)` is only correct when both of its inputs are guaranteed to fit in `n` bits. The signal `nonce` is attacker-controlled and is never range-checked. A malicious prover can supply a value like `p - 1` (the field modulus minus one); inside `LessThan` this is interpreted through its bit decomposition and the high-order arithmetic underflows, so the comparator outputs `1` even though `nonce` is enormously larger than `EPOCH_KEY_NONCE_PER_EPOCH`. The constraint `nonceCheck.out === 1` is then satisfied for an out-of-range nonce, letting the prover use an invalid epoch-key nonce. This is a classic missing-range-check on a comparator input.

Fix: Range-check `nonce` with `Num2Bits` so it provably fits in the bit width `LessThan` assumes before performing the comparison, preventing the overflow exploit.

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.0;

include "circomlib/circuits/bitify.circom";
include "circomlib/circuits/comparators.circom";

template EpochKeyLite(FIELD_COUNT) {
    var EPOCH_KEY_NONCE_PER_EPOCH = 3;

    signal input nonce;
    signal output out;

    // FIX: constrain nonce to fit in the bit width LessThan(8) assumes, so the
    // comparison cannot be made to overflow with values like p-1.
    component nonceBits = Num2Bits(8);
    nonceBits.in <== nonce;

    component nonceCheck = LessThan(8);
    nonceCheck.in[0] <== nonce;
    nonceCheck.in[1] <== EPOCH_KEY_NONCE_PER_EPOCH;
    nonceCheck.out === 1;

    out <== nonce;
}

component main = EpochKeyLite(255);
```

Audit this code for vulnerabilities:

```rust
fn fill_hash_tbl_custom(&self, region: &mut Region<'_, Fp>) -> Result<usize, Error> {
    let config = &self.config;

    config.s_custom.enable(region, 0)?;
    // all zero row
    // ... assign dummy zero cells at offset 0 ...

    if self.mpt_only {
        return Ok(1);
    }
    config.s_custom.enable(region, 1)?;

    // custom row at offset 1 (nil message hash), only reached in non-mpt mode
    // ... assign custom inputs / nil_msg_hash / header_mark at offset 1 ...

    Ok(2)
}
```

Vulnerability: mpt_only short-circuit enables the custom gate on an unwritten row, over-constraining the Poseidon hash table

Explanation: The custom gate `s_custom` enforces that `hash_inp[0]`, `hash_inp[1]` and `control` are all zero on every row where the selector is on (the "custom row" gate multiplies `s_enable` by each of these advice values). In `fill_hash_tbl_custom`, when `self.mpt_only` is true the function returns `Ok(1)` *before* the `config.s_custom.enable(region, 1)?` call, so the second custom row is never enabled and offset 1 stays a normal hashable row. But the ordering here is inverted relative to the intent: the code as written reaches `config.s_custom.enable(region, 1)?` only in the non-mpt path, while the all-zero padding semantics it is supposed to enforce belong to the mpt-only path. Because the selector enable and the early `return` are in the wrong order, when `mpt_only` is true the custom gate is left enabled over a row whose inputs are real (non-zero) hash inputs, forcing `hash_inp[0] == hash_inp[1] == control == 0`. Any genuine hashing attempt with non-zero inputs then fails verification, breaking completeness of the circuit even for honest provers. This is an over-constraint / completeness bug rooted in the incorrect ordering of the `mpt_only` check and the `s_custom.enable` call.

Fix: Reorder the two statements so the custom gate is enabled before the `mpt_only` short-circuit, ensuring the zero-constraint applies to the intended padding row and the early return happens after the selector is set.

```rust
fn fill_hash_tbl_custom(&self, region: &mut Region<'_, Fp>) -> Result<usize, Error> {
    let config = &self.config;

    config.s_custom.enable(region, 0)?;
    // all zero row
    // ... assign dummy zero cells at offset 0 ...

    config.s_custom.enable(region, 1)?;
    if self.mpt_only {
        return Ok(1);
    }

    // custom row at offset 1 (nil message hash), only reached in non-mpt mode
    // ... assign custom inputs / nil_msg_hash / header_mark at offset 1 ...

    Ok(2)
}
```

Audit this code for vulnerabilities:

```rust
fn configure(cb: &mut ConstraintBuilder<F>) -> Self {
    let opcode = cb.query_cell();
    let dest_offset = cb.query_cell_phase2();
    let data_offset = cb.query_word_rlc();
    let size = cb.query_word_rlc();

    // Pop dest_offset, offset, length from stack
    cb.stack_pop(dest_offset.expr());
    cb.stack_pop(data_offset.expr());
    cb.stack_pop(size.expr());

    // ... call context lookups for last_callee_id / return_data_offset / return_data_size ...

    // copy overflow check: offset + size <= return_data_size
    let in_bound_check = RangeCheckGadget::construct(
        cb,
        return_data_size.expr()
            - (from_bytes::expr(&data_offset.cells) + from_bytes::expr(&size.cells)),
    );

    // ... memory copy, gas, state transition ...
}
```

Vulnerability: Nondeterministic execution of RETURNDATACOPY — success and out-of-bound error states are not disjoint

Explanation: `ReturnDataCopyGadget` constrains the success path with an in-bounds check `return_data_size - (data_offset + size)` via `RangeCheckGadget`, asserting `data_offset + size <= return_data_size`. The separate `ErrorReturnDataOutOfBoundGadget` handles the error path where `data_offset + size > return_data_size`. However, nothing forces these two execution states to be mutually exclusive over their shared witness inputs: the success gadget's range check is the only thing distinguishing the two, and a malicious prover who supplies inputs that satisfy the boundary can choose *either* gadget for the same step. Because the success state is not constrained to be disjoint from the error state, the prover can arbitrarily route a step that should halt with an out-of-bound error into the successful `RETURNDATACOPY` gadget (or vice versa), making circuit execution nondeterministic and allowing it to diverge from correct EVM execution. This is a soundness bug: the choice of execution path is under-constrained.

Fix: Add constraints making the successful execution state disjoint from the error execution state so the two cannot both be valid for the same inputs — e.g. bind the in-bounds condition to a single boolean that the success and error gadgets each require to a fixed value, and audit the other error states for the same disjointness property.

```rust
fn configure(cb: &mut ConstraintBuilder<F>) -> Self {
    let opcode = cb.query_cell();
    let dest_offset = cb.query_cell_phase2();
    let data_offset = cb.query_word_rlc();
    let size = cb.query_word_rlc();

    cb.stack_pop(dest_offset.expr());
    cb.stack_pop(data_offset.expr());
    cb.stack_pop(size.expr());

    // ... call context lookups for last_callee_id / return_data_offset / return_data_size ...

    // Make success and error states disjoint: success REQUIRES in-bounds.
    // `is_in_bound` is the LtGadget result for (data_offset + size) <= return_data_size;
    // the ErrorReturnDataOutOfBoundGadget requires the negation, so the prover
    // cannot select either path for the same inputs.
    let is_in_bound = LtGadget::construct(
        cb,
        from_bytes::expr(&data_offset.cells) + from_bytes::expr(&size.cells),
        return_data_size.expr() + 1.expr(),
    );
    cb.require_equal(
        "RETURNDATACOPY success requires offset + size <= return_data_size",
        is_in_bound.expr(),
        1.expr(),
    );

    // ... memory copy, gas, state transition ...
}
```

Audit this code for vulnerabilities:

```rust
// tx_id <= cum_num_txs
let tx_id_cmp_cum_num_txs = ComparatorChip::configure(
    meta,
    |meta| meta.query_fixed(q_enable, Rotation::cur()),
    |meta| meta.query_advice(tx_table.tx_id, Rotation::cur()),
    |meta| meta.query_advice(cum_num_txs, Rotation::cur()),
);

meta.create_gate("tx_id <= cum_num_txs", |meta| {
    let mut cb = BaseConstraintBuilder::default();

    let (lt_expr, eq_expr) = tx_id_cmp_cum_num_txs.expr(meta, None);
    cb.condition(is_block_num(meta), |cb| {
        cb.require_equal("lt or eq", sum::expr([lt_expr, eq_expr]), true.expr());
    });

    cb.gate(and::expr([
        meta.query_fixed(q_enable, Rotation::cur()),
        not::expr(meta.query_advice(is_padding_tx, Rotation::cur())),
    ]))
});
```

Vulnerability: Highest tx_id is not constrained to equal cum_num_txs in the Tx Circuit

Explanation: The `tx_id_cmp_cum_num_txs` comparator and the `tx_id <= cum_num_txs` gate only enforce a one-sided inequality: for every non-padding transaction, `tx_id` must be less-than-or-equal to `cum_num_txs` (the cumulative number of txs up to the block, looked up from the block table). There is no constraint that the *largest* `tx_id` actually reaches `cum_num_txs`. As a result a malicious prover can set `cum_num_txs` much larger than the real number of transactions while every individual `tx_id <= cum_num_txs` still holds, so the block's claimed transaction count diverges from the set of transactions actually present in the TxTable. This breaks the specification's invariant that the last non-padding tx's `tx_id` equals `cum_num_txs`, leading to inconsistent transaction processing — a soundness violation.

Fix: Add a constraint enforcing that the `tx_id` of the last non-padding transaction equals `cum_num_txs`, so the highest tx_id must match the cumulative count rather than merely being bounded by it.

```rust
// tx_id <= cum_num_txs
let tx_id_cmp_cum_num_txs = ComparatorChip::configure(
    meta,
    |meta| meta.query_fixed(q_enable, Rotation::cur()),
    |meta| meta.query_advice(tx_table.tx_id, Rotation::cur()),
    |meta| meta.query_advice(cum_num_txs, Rotation::cur()),
);

meta.create_gate("tx_id <= cum_num_txs", |meta| {
    let mut cb = BaseConstraintBuilder::default();

    let (lt_expr, eq_expr) = tx_id_cmp_cum_num_txs.expr(meta, None);
    cb.condition(is_block_num(meta), |cb| {
        cb.require_equal("lt or eq", sum::expr([lt_expr, eq_expr.expr()]), true.expr());
        // The last non-padding tx (the one whose tx_id is the highest) must
        // make tx_id == cum_num_txs, so the claimed count cannot exceed the
        // actual set of transactions.
        cb.condition(meta.query_advice(is_last_tx, Rotation::cur()), |cb| {
            cb.require_equal(
                "highest tx_id == cum_num_txs",
                eq_expr,
                true.expr(),
            );
        });
    });

    cb.gate(and::expr([
        meta.query_fixed(q_enable, Rotation::cur()),
