Vulnerability: Plonky3 FRI verifier: missing folding randomness and final-polynomial degree check

Explanation: This is the OpenVM/Plonky3 native FRI verifier (Plonky3 advisory GHSA-f69f-5fx9-w9r9). Two soundness flaws are present. (1) When rolling a lower-degree polynomial's reduced opening into `folded_eval` it is added directly (`folded_eval + reduced_opening`) with no randomizing factor. Plonky3 folds with a `beta^2` coefficient so that polynomials of different degrees are combined under independent random weights; without it a malicious prover can choose openings whose high-degree parts cancel against the folded value, so a high-degree (non-low-degree) committed polynomial still passes the FRI low-degree test. (2) `verify_query` (and the surrounding FRI verify) never asserts that `proof.final_poly` has the length dictated by `config.final_poly_len()`, so the prover may supply a higher-than-expected-degree final polynomial that escapes the degree bound. Together these break FRI soundness, letting a prover open a polynomial it has not committed to of the claimed low degree, and thus forge proofs for the recursive / on-chain verifier.

Fix: Apply the Plonky3 fixes: precompute `betas_squared` and fold the reduced openings in with the `beta^2` randomness term, and enforce `proof.final_poly.len() == config.final_poly_len()` (fixed in OpenVM v1.2.0, PR #1703).

