```cpp
// step_exec.cpp -- BodyStep of the rv32im circuit.
// Some RISC-V instructions take several zkVM cycles. The FIRST cycle sets a
// "major mode" that tells the next cycle how to continue the instruction.
void step_exec(ExecContext& ctx, /* ... */ size_t cycle) {
  // Decode the current cycle's major mode from the layout.
  Val major = LOAD(LAYOUT_LOOKUP(layout, body.major), 0);

  // Dispatch on `major` to continue the (possibly multi-cycle) instruction.
  // The continuation arm for a multi-cycle instruction is selected purely from
  // `major` read out of THIS cycle's witness.
  if (to_size_t(major == kMajorECall)) {
    step_exec_ecall_continue(ctx, /* ... */);
  } else if (to_size_t(major == kMajorMemIO)) {
    step_exec_memio_continue(ctx, /* ... */);
  } else {
    step_exec_normal(ctx, /* ... */);
  }

  // BUG: there is NO constraint tying this cycle's `major` to a mode that the
  // PREVIOUS instruction actually committed to continue. No `nextMajor` register
  // is carried across cycles, and no majorMux / extern_isTrap / extern_setUserMode
  // enforces that a continuation mode was legitimately entered.
}
```

Vulnerability: Multi-step instruction major-mode not constrained across cycles

Explanation: This is the rv32im `BodyStep`/`step_exec` of the RISC Zero zkVM. Certain instructions execute over several cycles; the first cycle sets a `major_mode` telling the next cycle how to continue. Prior to v1.1.0 the circuit selected the continuation arm purely from the `major` value read out of the current cycle's witness, with no constraint ensuring that the previous instruction had definitively committed to that continuation mode. There is no `nextMajor` register carried across cycles, no `majorMux`/`majorSelect` cross-cycle check, and no `extern_isTrap`/`extern_setUserMode` mode validation. This is an under-constrained state: a malicious prover can pick a `major_mode` for a cycle that was never legitimately entered by the prior step, splicing the continuation half of a multi-cycle instruction onto an unrelated state and producing a valid-looking proof of an execution that never happened (soundness break across the 16+ multi-step dispatch sites in the body).

Fix: Carry a `nextMajor` register in `BodyStep` set by each cycle and constrain the next cycle's `major` to equal it via a `majorMux`/`majorSelect` component, and add the `extern_isTrap` / `extern_setUserMode` mode-validation externs so continuation modes can only be entered legitimately. Fixed in v1.1.0 (commit 1e6ca468f); use >= v1.1.1.

```cpp
// step_exec.cpp -- BodyStep of the rv32im circuit (fixed).
void step_exec(ExecContext& ctx, /* ... */ size_t cycle) {
  Val major = LOAD(LAYOUT_LOOKUP(layout, body.major), 0);

  // Cross-cycle constraint: this cycle's `major` must equal the `nextMajor` that
  // the PREVIOUS cycle committed to. majorMux/majorSelect bind them together.
  if (cycle > 0) {
    Val prevNextMajor = LOAD(LAYOUT_LOOKUP(prevLayout, body.nextMajor), 0);
    EQZ((major - prevNextMajor), "major mode must match previous cycle's committed nextMajor");
  }

  // Validate trap / user-mode transitions through dedicated externs.
  Val isTrap = INVOKE_EXTERN(ctx, isTrap, major);
  INVOKE_EXTERN(ctx, setUserMode, major, isTrap);

  // Dispatch, and have THIS cycle commit the mode the next cycle must continue in.
  Val nextMajor = exec_MajorMux(ctx, major, LAYOUT_LOOKUP(layout, body.majorMux));
  STORE(LAYOUT_LOOKUP(layout, body.nextMajor), nextMajor);

  if (to_size_t(major == kMajorECall)) {
    step_exec_ecall_continue(ctx, /* ... */);
  } else if (to_size_t(major == kMajorMemIO)) {
    step_exec_memio_continue(ctx, /* ... */);
  } else {
    step_exec_normal(ctx, /* ... */);
  }
}
```

